Found 2026-09-30 by the Net::Async::Kubernetes live check (its card 73) against kube-apiserver v1.36.1. A watch started without resourceVersion first sends synthetic ADDED events for existing objects, sorted by name, each with its own (older) resourceVersion. watch() returns the last event's version; a caller that resumes from it after a quiet server timeout gets history replayed (state goes backwards, duplicate ADDED, DELETED for objects gone before the watch started). Likely fix, same as proposed for Net::Async::Kubernetes: allowWatchBookmarks=true and keep the bookmark's version (v1.36 sends one right after the initial events), or list-then-watch. Not a regression; decide together with nak card 73.

2026-10-18 06:04 watch(): allowWatchBookmarks=true, BOOKMARK swallowed + sets resume point, no start rv -> event rvs ignored until first BOOKMARK, 410 on request with rv -> plain 'Watch expired' croak. Adjusted t/10_watch.t (pass start rv) and t/49_query_encoding.t (new query param) - both asserted the old behaviour. watch POD needs update.

